WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.060
Even by the standards of superhero comics,
Supergirl's long history is truly weird.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:10.411
From her ill-fated romances to being obliterated
from existence and replaced by a blob of protoplasm

00:00:10.411 --> 00:00:15.179
that used to sleep with Lex Luthor, here are
the most unbelievably bizarre pieces of Supergirl's

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.340
buck wild history.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.540
The single weirdest thing about Supergirl
might be that she didn't show up for a full

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:22.600
20 years after the creation of Superman.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:29.390
Despite introducing Superboy in 1944 and Krypto
the Superdog in 1955, DC Comics wouldn't get

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:32.660
around to even attempting Supergirl for another
three years after that.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:36.900
When they finally did get around to this whole
"Super-Girl" thing, she was originally introduced

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.260
as a one-off character.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:42.980
Not only was this version not Superman's cousin,
she actually died at the end of the story,

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:44.100
and was never seen again.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:48.660
The first version, the one with the hyphen
in her name, showed up in 1958's Superman

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:54.800
#123, when Superman's Pal Jimmy Olsen found
a magic genie belt and wished her into existence.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.160
After a few encounters in which she and Superman
kept getting in each other's way, Super-Girl

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.890
wound up exposing herself to Kryptonite and
then begging Jimmy to end her hellish existence.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:04.880
Pretty dark.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:09.340
Despite her unfortunate end, this Super-Girl
served as a prototype for the Girl of Steel

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:13.260
that would be introduced the following year
after readers responded well to the idea.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:18.140
Like Super-Girl, Kara's relative inexperience
with her powers would be a frequent plot point,

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.280
and visually, they were almost indistinguishable.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.260
Super-Girl's costume was not only similar
to what readers would see, it's virtually

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:28.340
identical to the one Melissa Benoist would
wear on the Supergirl TV show almost 60 years

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:28.940
later.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:36.000
In 1959, Action Comics #252 told the story
of "The Supergirl From Krypton," and introduced

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.620
readers to Supergirl for real.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:42.740
This time, it was Kara Zor-El, Superman's
cousin whose family had survived the destruction

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:47.490
of Krypton when their home, Argo City, was
blasted into space as a single intact chunk

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:48.140
of the planet.

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:53.060
Unfortunately, the explosion turned Argo City
into Kryptonite, poisoning the inhabitants

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:57.350
and leading Zor-El to follow in his brother's
footsteps by packing his kid into a rocket

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:58.440
and shooting her to Earth.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.120
All things considered, that's a pretty plausible
origin story by Superman standards.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:07.080
The weirdest part of the story is that Kara
gets a pretty cold reception from her cousin.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:11.600
For two decades' worth of stories, Superman
had believed himself to be the only survivor

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:13.790
of his home planet, alone in the universe.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:18.739
Finally, he has a connection to Krypton, someone
else who has lost her entire family and culture.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.920
At first, he's welcoming... and then she asks
if she can come live with him.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.310
Superman's response is to refuse on the basis
of his secret identity being in jeopardy.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:30.040
In other words, he doesn't want her cramping
his style at his bachelor pad.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.420
Instead, he gives her a wig and drops her
off at an orphanage.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:37.260
That's how Superman deals with his only living
relative in the universe.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:42.020
By and large, Supergirl's adventures in the
'50s and '60s tended to follow the same pattern

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:43.800
as Superman's, at least at first.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:49.250
Her first major love interest was Dick Malverne,
a classmate who, like Lois Lane and Lana Lang

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:53.520
before him, suspected Kara's secret identity
and tried to trick her into revealing herself.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.000
As time went on, though, Supergirl's love
life got truly weird.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:01.780
In addition to a long-running romance with
a cyborg from the future, Supergirl's primary

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:03.720
romantic interest was a horse.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.840
Specifically, Comet the Super-Horse.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.440
Just wait, it gets crazier.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:11.680
Comet had actually been a centaur back in
Hercules' time who, through a combination

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:16.000
of curses and bizarre events, wound up living
in space for a couple thousand years as a

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.680
bulletproof flying horse who still had sentience.

00:03:19.129 --> 00:03:23.120
Whenever a specific comet came close to Earth,
though, the magic would turn him fully human

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:23.940
for a few days.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:28.220
So naturally, he kept all of this secret from
Supergirl, assumed the human identity of ace

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:32.500
rodeo rider "Bronco" Bill Starr, and dated
Supergirl without ever telling her who he

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:33.000
was.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.340
No romantic relationship in comics has ever
even gotten close to being that weird.

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:42.680
As you might've heard, 1979 saw the release
of Superman: The Movie, which was kind of

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:43.300
a big deal.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:47.660
With Christopher Reeve's performance as the
Man of Steel, audiences loved the movie, which

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:51.000
launched DC's flagship character into a full-on
franchise.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.760
By the mid-'80s and the release of Superman
III, enthusiasm for the Superman films had

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.940
waned a bit, but they were still successful
enough to warrant a spinoff.

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:03.560
Thus Supergirl, which attempted to recapture
the magic and expand Superman's movie universe

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:05.580
and didn't quite manage to pull it off.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:09.700
While Helen Slater received a fair amount
of praise in the title role, the movie itself

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:10.640
was pretty weird.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:14.220
For one thing, it had very little connection
to the other Superman films.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:18.769
The only actor to appear in both was Marc
McClure as Jimmy Olsen, without even a cameo

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:19.420
from Reeve.

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:21.440
Even stranger was the plot.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:26.150
The film pitted Supergirl against Faye Dunaway
as an aspiring witch named Selena who gets

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:30.509
her hands on a Kryptonian artifact called
the "Omegahedron" and tries to use it to summon

00:04:30.509 --> 00:04:31.000
demons.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.200
Still, Slater's wide-eyed earnestness in the
part has its appeal, and her connection to

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:39.150
the character has remained strong throughout
the decades since Slater would go on to play

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:44.139
Superman's mother, Lara, on Smallville, Supergirl's
adopted mom Eliza Danvers on Supergirl, and

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:47.180
even voiced Superman's mom, Martha Kent, in
an animated film.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.840
In 1986, DC Comics launched an ambitious attempt
to restructure their entire universe with

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:55.580
a yearlong event called Crisis on Infinite
Earths.

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:59.400
The main idea was to eliminate some of the
aspects that readers had come to regard as

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:05.080
hokey and overcomplicated, condensing confusing
timelines down to a single, streamlined universe.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:09.550
Given that he had a longer and more convoluted
history than anyone else on the DC Comics

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:13.300
roster, it's not surprising that the Superman
books went through the biggest overhaul.

00:05:13.669 --> 00:05:18.319
Along with recasting Lex Luthor as a deeply
corrupt billionaire businessman, the big changes

00:05:18.319 --> 00:05:22.800
came in the form of a new origin for Clark
Kent that re-established him as the true Last

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:23.720
Son of Krypton.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:28.740
In the new continuity, he was now the only
survivor of the planet's explosion and eliminated

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.780
the idea that he had his full powers before
he was an adult.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:37.140
That meant no Superboy, no Krypto the Superdog,
no Comet the Super-Horse, and no Supergirl.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:41.400
To give the character an ending rather than
just writing her out of the books, Crisis

00:05:41.409 --> 00:05:46.139
on Infinite Earths saw Kara sacrificing her
life in order to save Superman and, with him,

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:48.040
countless billions of innocent lives.

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:52.520
The thing is, the way the effects of Crisis
were resolved in the books, she wasn't a dead

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:54.180
hero who was remembered in honor.

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:57.740
Instead, in the new DC Universe, she'd simply
never existed.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:02.320
There was, however, an epilogue to Kara's
story that cropped up in a pretty unexpected

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:02.820
place.

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:08.080
In 1989's Christmas with the Superheroes,
a holiday anthology with a handful of stories,

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:11.980
the ghostly Deadman lamented the fact that
while he could briefly possess the living,

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.800
he'd always leave them with no memory of his
heroic deeds.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.880
While he dealt with those feelings, a ghostly
woman showed up and told him that heroism

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:20.260
isn't done for recognition.

00:06:20.629 --> 00:06:24.319
In the final panels of the story, the woman
gives her name as Kara, and while it didn't

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:27.100
mean anything to Deadman, it meant a lot to
the readers.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:31.779
Crisis had done its level best to get rid
of concepts like Superboy, Supergirl, and

00:06:31.779 --> 00:06:35.740
Krypto, but if you're familiar with superhero
stories at all, it probably won't surprise

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:38.520
you to learn that literally all of it eventually
came back.

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:42.980
Even putting aside the nostalgia that a new
generation of creators had for their favorites,

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:47.289
copyrights on characters like Supergirl are
very valuable, and if they're not being used,

00:06:47.289 --> 00:06:49.620
they can fall out of the control of the companies
that own them.

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:52.540
Supergirl's return to the comics page isn't
surprising.

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:56.540
The fact that she did it in less than two
years, however, is pretty surprising.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:06:58.760
To be fair, this wasn't Kara Zor-El.

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:03.619
Instead, this Supergirl was a shape-shifting
"protoplasmic matrix" created in an alternate

00:07:03.619 --> 00:07:08.449
pocket dimension by a morally good Lex Luthor,
who made her look like his dead ex-girlfriend

00:07:08.449 --> 00:07:11.700
and gave her all of Superman's powers plus
turning invisible.

00:07:12.029 --> 00:07:16.280
She then came to the regular DC Earth and
moved in with Superman's parents, who called

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:19.160
her "Mae," short for "Matrix," and she became
the new Supergirl.

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:20.620
"Woah."

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:26.179
She then moved in with Lex Luthor II, who
was actually the regular, evil Lex Luthor

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:30.040
who had transplanted his brain into a clone
body and told everyone that he was his own

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:30.540
son.

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:35.020
A few years later, she symbiotically bonded
with a dead teenager and split into a good

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:38.540
Matrix and an evil Matrix, and might have
also been an angel.

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:40.740
Good thing we had that Crisis thing, huh?

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:45.440
This new stuff was way easier for new readers
to understand than "she's Superman's cousin."

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:49.879
If you think the post-Crisis Supergirl was
complicated, get ready, because we're about

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:51.340
to talk about Power Girl.

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:55.119
Right from the start, she's a little more
complicated, but the short version is that

00:07:55.119 --> 00:07:59.759
before Crisis, DC's multiverse contained a
dimension called Earth-2 where they stuck

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:03.480
all the characters from the '40s who had been
rebooted in the '50s and '60s.

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:07.599
Whenever they showed up, they were always
presented as being a decade or two older than

00:08:07.599 --> 00:08:11.849
their mainstream "Earth-1" counterparts, giving
a home to characters like the original Flash,

00:08:11.849 --> 00:08:13.260
Green Lantern, and Superman.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:18.900
Superman still had a cousin on that Earth,
but Earth-2's Kara Zor-L didn't go by Supergirl.

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:23.260
Instead, this older version went by Power
Girl, with a completely different costume.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:27.780
For some reason, Power Girl was considered
to be more necessary for the post-Crisis DC

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:30.980
Universe, and managed to stick around once
the universe was streamlined.

00:08:31.589 --> 00:08:35.960
The thing is, Power Girl was an alternate
version of a character who no longer existed,

00:08:35.969 --> 00:08:40.300
from a universe that no longer existed, whose
entire reason for being had been completely

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:40.980
eliminated.

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:45.360
The post-Crisis Power Girl could no longer
be Superman's cousin, and creators spent the

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:48.000
next 20 years trying to figure out what her
deal was.

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:52.350
The first big solution was that she was actually
the granddaughter of a wizard from the lost

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:56.940
continent of Atlantis who put her in a magic
crystal for 45,000 years.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:01.430
Then she got pregnant and gave birth to a
magic rapidly-aging baby who was never seen

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:01.920
again.

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:06.160
Then she was a major character in a team book
that, when it got canceled, was revealed to

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:08.640
have been a fantasy drawn in a child's coloring
book.

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:10.900
Then she was just Superman's cousin again.

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:11.440
Simple!

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:13.900
"I missed out on the whole thing to be honest
with you.

00:09:13.910 --> 00:09:15.540
I was confused by it.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:18.780
I was confused by the whole thing, if if I were
being honest.”

00:09:19.340 --> 00:09:23.960
If the concept of Matrix-Supergirl wasn't
weird enough, 1996 saw the launch of a new

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:27.560
Supergirl series, in which Supergirl gained
a new secret identity.

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:32.410
This time, it was Linda Danvers, a young woman
who had unfortunately been kidnapped and sacrificed

00:09:32.410 --> 00:09:35.260
to the devil before her first comic ever even
started.

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:40.100
Matrix found her body and, overwhelmed with
grief, bonded with her, merging her protoplasmic

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:43.880
form and personality with Linda's, becoming
a new Supergirl in the process.

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:49.360
A little ways into the run, Supergirl spontaneously
developed the ability to sprout fiery wings,

00:09:49.379 --> 00:09:54.269
and the book began to feature Wallace Johnson,
a.k.a. Wally the God-Boy, a boy who claimed

00:09:54.269 --> 00:09:55.020
he was God.

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:59.100
It eventually turned out that Linda was an
"Earth-born angel," and that bonding with

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:03.819
Matrix's super-powered protoplasm had allowed
her human form to contain this divine essence.

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:08.500
At this point, Supergirl was an angel who
was also a teenage devil worshipper who was

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:13.220
also a blob from another dimension, who was
also somehow still connected to Superman.

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:17.339
It won't surprise you to learn that after
a few years of this, they got rid of this

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:21.200
Supergirl and introduced another one who was
just Superman's cousin from Krypton.

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:25.360
"Her name is Kara Zor-El, from Krypton.

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:26.820
She's my cousin."

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:28.940
